What is Aircraft Secondary Surveillance Radar (SSR)?
Secondary Surveillance Radar (SSR) is an advanced air traffic control technology that identifies and tracks aircraft through active interrogation of transponders installed onboard. Unlike primary radar systems that rely solely on passive radio wave reflections, SSR establishes two-way communication with aircraft, receiving critical flight data including altitude, unique identification codes, and speed. This technology has become indispensable for modern airspace management, providing air traffic controllers with accurate situational awareness in increasingly congested skies.
The SSR system operates by transmitting interrogation signals that prompt aircraft transponders to respond with digital data packets. This cooperative surveillance method enables precise tracking even in adverse weather conditions or terrain-challenged environments where primary radar performance degrades. Modern SSR systems are evolving beyond basic identification, integrating capabilities like Mode S selective addressing and Automatic Dependent Surveillance-Broadcast (ADS-B) compatibility.
